      "quote": "shall publicly disclose each person who contributed more than $1,000 toward those expenditures",
      "mechanism": "Adds a new tracking and reporting requirement for corporations that cross the political expenditure threshold.",
      "certainty": "stated"
    }
  ],
  "analysis": [
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q1",
      "answer": "Currently, corporations only report the total amount they spend on independent political expenditures. Under this measure, if they spend over $10,000, they must also publicly disclose the identities of individuals or entities who contributed more than $1,000 toward that spending.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q2",
      "answer": "Corporations will face new administrative and compliance costs to track donor identities and report them to the relevant public agency. The state may face minor administrative costs to process, store, and publish the new disclosure forms.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q3",
      "answer": "Corporations and their large donors bear the cost of lost privacy and increased compliance paperwork. The public, media, watchdog organizations, and opposing political campaigns benefit from increased transparency regarding the financial backers of corporate political spending.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "answer": "Donors who wish to remain anonymous may split their donations to stay under the $1,000 limit, route funds through alternative entities not covered by this specific disclosure rule, or reduce their political giving entirely. Corporations may choose to limit their independent political expenditures to $10,000 or less to avoid triggering the disclosure requirement.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q5",
      "answer": "The measure addresses the issue of \"dark money,\" where the ultimate source of political spending is obscured by routing funds through corporate entities. By forcing disclosure of underlying donors, the mechanism directly targets this information gap, though it may incentivize donors to find new, unregulated channels to maintain anonymity.",
      "disputed": false
    },
    {
      "clause_id": "§1",
      "question_id": "Q6",
      "answer": "Journalists, political opponents, and advocacy groups gain information they can use to scrutinize, track, or publicly pressure large donors and the corporations they fund.",
      "disputed": false
    }
  ],
